In the case of MIP-203-M, the response to the loading of solvent molecules exhibits a combined effect observed in MIP-203-F and MIP-203-S. As shown in Fig. 2c, solvent molecules with large sizes and branches, such as DMF, have a lesser effect on the MOF structure expansion, mainly due to the local bond distortion/bending and steric hindrance of the malate linker. Smaller solvent molecules could diffuse more easily into the MOF pore. In other words, a good structural swelling flexibility of the framework associated with an appropriate local bond distortion/bending towards guest molecules has been achieved in MIP-203-M.
In situ infrared (IR) spectroscopy was employed to probe the interactions between the guest solvent molecules and the framework. DMF was chosen as the representative solvent to study its adsorption in the selected MOFs, due to the structural responses of these MOFs to DMF solvent showing significant variation (Fig.2). A vapor pressure of 4 Torr of DMF was introduced into the activated MOFs samples for ${\sim}5~\mathrm{min}$ to reach the adsorption equilibrium with the adsorbed DMF was characterized by a carbonyl stretching band $\scriptstyle\v{v}(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})$ at $1676~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , shifted by $39~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ from the center of the gas phase band (Fig. 3 and Figure S8). A comparison of the intensities of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{v(C=O)}$ band at $1676~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in IR spectra of Fig. 3 indicates that the uptakes of adsorbed DMF in these three samples exhibits the following trend: MIP-203-F > N $1\mathrm{IP-203-M>MIP-203-S}$ .
