To check the structural response of the MOF framework in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ guest molecules, in situ PXRD data along the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under various pressures was collected on the three MIP-203 samples at room temperature. As shown in Figure 5, both activated MIP-203-F and MIP-203-M structures could easily swell back to the open form under a low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure (between 200-300 mbar), highlighting their swelling flexibility. Further increasing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure did not have any notable effects on the samples’ structural expansion. However, the activated MIP-203-S sample remained in its closely packed configuration throughout the entire range of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressures applied. Even high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure (6 bar) was not able to reopen the framework, which is consistent with its lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake compared to those of MIP-203-F and MIP-203-M under the same condition.
To evaluate the adsorption affinity, isosteric heats of adsorption $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\right)$ were calculated with the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at 273 K, 298 K and $303\mathrm{~K~}$ in the pressure range of 0-1 bar. At zero coverage, the Qst values are $32\mathrm{KJ\mol^{-1}}$ , $34\mathrm{KJ\mol^{-1}}$ and $33\mathrm{KJ\mol^{-1}}$ for MIP-203-F, MIP-203-S and MIP-203-M, respectively. The working capacities of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption $(\mathrm{Wc}(1{-}0.1\ \mathrm{bar}))$ for three MIP-203 samples were calculated to be 1.38, 1.04 and $1.15\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ for MIP-203-F, MIP-203-S and MIP-203-M, respectively, at 273 K. The corresponding values at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ are 1.35, 0.47 and $1.09\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ . It is interesting to note that the working capacities for both the MIP-203-F and MIP-203-M compounds showed far less dependence on the working temperature, while the working capacity of MIP-203-S exhibited dramatic sensitivity towards the applied temperature, decreasing by more than half when the testing temperature increased from 273 K to 298 K. Furthermore, Ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST)52-53 was applied to evaluate the adsorption selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ for MIP-203s when a binary gaseous mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}{:}\mathrm{N}_{2}{=}15{:}85$ (v:v) at $298\mathrm{K}$ was considered (Fig. S14). The corresponding selectivities were calculated to be 15, 34 and 51 for
