It is worth noting that MIP-203-S shows a selectivity twice as high as that of MIP-203-F, highlighting the linker softness effect that interferes nitrogen from entering the pores. However, MIP-203-M outperforms MIP-203-S in terms of selectivity by a margin of $50\%$ . The above comparison of the working capacity and adsorptive selectivity between the three MIP-203 compounds suggests that the introduction of proper local softness into a structurally flexible MOF framework would result in a favorable combination of accessible porosity and structural dynamics, leading to an optimal balance for selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation in terms of working capacity and selectivity.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we report a series of flexible isostructural $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs synthesized based on natural C4 linkers (MIP-203s), in which the structural dynamics can be engineered based on the differences in freedom of linker bond stretching and rotation between rigid fumarate and soft succinate. Comprehensive characterizations of structural responses to diverse external stimuli, including thermal changes as well as liquid and gaseous guest molecules, have been conducted to correlate the corresponding linker’s flexibility with MOF dynamics. Directed by this established correlation, MIP-203-M, with a proper multivariate flexibility that efficiently combines swelling and linker distortion/bending, was evaluated for the adsorptive separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . It not only exhibits a good working capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption under the working condition benefiting from the swelling MIP-203-F framework, but also a significantly enhanced selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ compared to those of the $-\mathrm{F}$ and $-\mathsf{S}$ forms, owing to the linker stretching and rotational flexibility generated from the soft succinate skeleton. In principle, this strategy could be applied to the separation of other chemical mixtures, including liquid and gaseous phases. Therefore, it serves as a suitable example that engineering the structural flexibility of MOF materials could constitute a promising and versatile strategy for improving MOF separation performance, or even realizing new applications.
